The build passes. Here's a summary of the fix:

## Bug Analysis

**Symptom:** When creating a new note, entering a title and content, pressing the save icon, then pressing the back arrow - the note is NOT created. But if the user skips the save and just presses the back arrow, the note IS created.

**Root Cause:** In `DocumentActivity.onOptionsItemSelected()`, the back arrow handler had this code:

```java
case android.R.id.home: {
    if (isTaskRoot()) {
        startActivity(new Intent(this, MainActivity.class));  // BUG: creates duplicate MainActivity
    }
    super.onBackPressed();
    return true;
}
```

When the user presses the back arrow, this `startActivity()` call creates a **new** MainActivity instance. Then `finish()` destroys DocumentActivity, but the user sees the **new** MainActivity instance (with an unrefreshed/empty list) instead of returning to the **original** MainActivity that's still in the back stack with the refreshed list.

**The Fix:** Remove the unnecessary `startActivity()` call. Just call `super.onBackPressed()` directly:

```java
case android.R.id.home: {
    super.onBackPressed();
    return true;
}
```

This way, when DocumentActivity finishes, the original MainActivity (which is behind it in the back stack) is displayed with the refreshed note list.

**Verification:** The build compiles successfully with this change.

